WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.980
[Cheering]

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.900
MATT: What up, crew?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.860
[Laughter]

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.320
Yeah, get the door, door boy!

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.000
Just put this back on to cover up the man-boobs.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.040
[Whistle from audience]

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:20.920
Thank you!

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.420
She's up there.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.900
Now, those of you who have known
what we've done for a while

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.760
will remember that a long time ago
we did some audio episodes.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.200
And I didn't know how much time we would
have left, exactly, at the end of this...

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.520
GARY: To be fair, we didn't know how long it'd get
before the cold bottles of piss hit the stage.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.360
What I like is that they're cold, which means
that people have pre-prepared them.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.600
[Laughter]
GARY: Yeah.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.040
Um, which means —

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.320
MATT: "This one's for piss..."

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.320
Er, which means that...

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.840
In front of me, I have some classic trivia questions!

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.720
[Cheering]
GARY: Ohhh, baby!

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.300
This is what they want!

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:03.780
"Finchley!"

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.280
And by classic, I mean they're from 1984.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.520
GARY: This s*** again!

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:10.560
[Laughter]

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.580
I am going to read out the answers;
all you have to do is guess the questions.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.700
Are you all ready?

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.340
God, remember the days when we did this
in a small, fart-filled room in Chester?

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.860
We had a really big curry one of the nights, and *he*...

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:31.960
He destroyed the room so badly we had to
open the outside door to let the maft out.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.500
That was the night I fell off your airbed and head-butted
your drum kit — oh, memories, memories.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.440
[Laughter]

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.340
We start...

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.120
...with Albert Blake Dick.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.300
Which Blake didn't make Blake's 7?

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.260
Because "Dick's 7" wouldn't sound... yeah.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.520
Should have seen the shape of the ship.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.300
It sounds like an order.
'Albert — blake Dick!'

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.560
What's 'blaking' someone?

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.920
I don't know, but I know what you use!

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:05.820
Heyy!

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.740
GARY: It's when Gareth Thomas comes up behind you —

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.780
TOM: NO.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.460
Two people got that reference.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.500
GARY: Thank you, both Blake's 7 fans in the audience.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.440
TOM: Now, see, this has actually been corrected since.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.120
The actual answer to this would be Thomas Edison...

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.160
GARY: Er, who shafted who out of some kind of patent?

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.080
Well, Dick was the one who named —
I can't say that seriously.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:27.960
[Laughter]

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.100
Blake Dick —
I can't say *that* seriously.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:31.920
Albert?

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.860
Albert was the one who named it.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.420
So we are looking for an invention.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:37.740
A tephelone!

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.500
Oh, no. We're looking for something
that you'd still use in an office today.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.160
GARY: Photocopier.
CHRIS: Chair.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.800
GARY: Window.
MATT: Electricity.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.260
Gary, you're very close.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.560
I'm looking for something a little bit — like,
photocopier, that was the Xerox Corporation...

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.560
What was an earlier version of that?

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:53.580
Typewriter.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.740
CHRIS: Carbon copying... thing... pad.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.520
Yeah, I'll give you the point. [DING]

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.240
We're looking for mimeograph machine.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.840
GARY and MATT: Mimeograph machine?

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.100
GARY: Sounds like a f***ing electro band.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.300
GARY: Yeah, I'm down with the kids.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.700
'Hello, we're Mimeograph Machine.'

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.300
CHRIS: [Beeping and booping]
GARY: 'Yeah. And we're here to annoy you pre-show.'

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.720
TOM: That's actually fair, isn't it.
GARY: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.620
We move on to 'the 35'.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.180
What yard line do you do some
bollocks at in American football?

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:21.340
[Laughter]

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.460
GARY: You don't get your bollocks out
in American football.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.040
Very dangerous.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.580
No, that's rugby, and that's afterwards.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:27.300
Ha ha!

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.200
Oh they do, though, don't they.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.960
You're absolutely right,
it is a yard line in American football...

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.900
Do you want to go any...

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.720
CHRIS: Line of scrimmage.
MATT: Is that the line you go past, that if you go...

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.840
That's where you... kick the thingy.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.620
[Applause]
GARY: This is why you never got that...

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.980
American football is one of the few
sports I sometimes watch.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.500
And I still don't know what I'm going to say.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.480
And yet, 'Is it the line you kick the thingy?'

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.420
The thing is, he's right. [DING]

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.160
[Applause]

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.760
That's what you get for watching the Superb Owl, isn't it.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:05.660
Yes.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:06.880
Super Bowel.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.620
Yeah. Hell of a show.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.380
Yeah. It's 'Which yard line do NFL teams kick off from?'

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.060
And before we get letters, this is from 1984.
It was moved ten years later.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:16.840
'Before we get letters'?

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.240
[Laughter]

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.560
MATT: Z! Q! R!

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.800
I'd quite like that, because the YouTube barrier to entry

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.120
would be quite a bit higher if you
had to comment by sending a letter.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.920
But on the other hand, I would
open my post in the morning —

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.060
You've got a letter opener as well!

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.780
[Laughter and applause]

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.440
Tom lives near an embassy.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.300
I like to think he does have a silver platter
that his post is delivered on.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.480
'Private and Confidential for you, sir...'
[Envelope slitting noises]

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.100
No, I just like the idea that I'm going to open a letter,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.780
and it's just going to be a vague insult on my person.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.680
All in caps.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.300
All in caps, obviously.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.980
That would be, yeah — YouTube comments by post.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.520
'u r crap'
Thank you!

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.760
That would be post with the word 'LOL' in it.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:01.700
Yeah.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.880
TOM: Your next one is... jai alai.
MATT: Thatcher!

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.140
MATT: The answer's almost always 'Thatcher'.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.660
TOM: Jai alai.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.780
CHRIS: Fastest ball speed, compared to lacrosse.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:11.400
[DING]

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.980
Absolutely spot on. Knocked it out of the park —

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.360
No, no, it bounces off the wall and comes back.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:17.620
[Laughter]

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.040
[Applause]

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.760
Your next one is... the Impossible Missions Force.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.440
What do they call us on a date night?

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.500
[Laughter]

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.640
Wow, that makes my answer even worse.
Ministry of Ungentlemanly Warfare?

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.560
Er, no, definitely not.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.140
MATT: 64 newtons.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.080
The Impossible Missions *Force*, right. Yeah.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.140
TOM: Good... good.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.080
Weak electromagnetic!
Same joke, but whatever.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.920
No. The abbreviation for that would be IMF.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.440
Where does the post get misdirected if you're
writing to the International Monetary Fund?

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:56.580
[Laughter]

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.040
Anyone who has seen a certain film
is likely to know the answer to this one.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.340
MATT: [Hums 'Mission: Impossible' bassline]

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.280
[DING]
GARY: Ahh.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:07.800
TOM: He's absolutely right. 'What's —'

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.640
['Mission: Impossible' melody]
Doodle-doo... doodle-doo...

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.220
Doodle-doo... doo-doo.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.380
TOM: He's absolutely ri —
MATT: Doodle-doo... doodle-doo...

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.780
[Applause]

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.160
Doodle-doo...!

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.400
Yeah. 'What was the name of the agency
from Mission: Impossible?'

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.580
The sound-man at the back, he's just
gone cross-eyed and his ears are going red.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.600
Your next one is... Intourist.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.940
What is the worst place to store a bottle of water?

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:38.380
[Laughter]

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:40.380
Thoop!

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:43.160
It's when it comes out without the cap on...

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.820
I store all my water lying down, anyway.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:49.500
Ohhh!

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.520
Where would a Yorkshireman put things 'in the ourist'?

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.840
TOM: In t' ourist! Yeah, okay.
GARY: In t'ourist.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.860
No, you would be saying this
in an accent as well, certainly.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.060
He just did!

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.000
Yeah, okay.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:04.060
And bear in mind, this is 1984.
They like questions about certain countries here.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:04.980
GARY: Russia!

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.460
TOM: Yes.
GARY: Ohoho.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:10.080
It is the state-run travel agency of the Soviet Union.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:10.760
Ahh.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.060
Mandatory tourism!

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.260
GARY: In Russia, travel does you.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:20.240
I can only imagine that you basically went up there,
said 'I'd like to go somewhere,' and they said, 'No.'

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:21.340
[Laughter]

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.280
It's like a railway station.
Big blinds they pull down and go,

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.900
'NO'... 'NO'... 'NO'... 'POLICE'.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.760
I would like to go on The Holiday.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.900
CHRIS: From what I understand from what I've read,

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.400
essentially you went there and you got
your week's allowance of... like, leave.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.920
But because it's leave from the country, in essence,

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.200
you got a week in this dacha,
at the place you were told to,

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.880
at the time you were told to be there.
And then you returned.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:45.560
Ahh.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:46.780
GARY: Butlins then.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:47.820
[Laughter]

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.480
Yeah, but the coats were red.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:50.620
Yeah.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:55.360
I have spent one brief holiday in Butlins,
and it is not all it's cracked up to be.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.100
And it's not cracked up to be much.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.480
I was going to say!

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.120
I tell you what, when I had a holiday
in Butlins, (A) it was brilliant,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.500
but (B) I was a bit worried by the barbed wire
and guard towers round the edge.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.700
GARY: I'm not joking, there were genuinely guard towers.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:09.740
Why...?

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:14.080
To stop — I'm not sure if it's to stop
other people getting in, or to stop us getting out.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.040
Why would they?

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:15.960
I don't know!

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.820
'Ooh, let's go break into Butlins!'

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:24.060
No, that is absolutely what kids in a small,
boring seaside town would do.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.840
You would absolutely go and break into the Butlins.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.280
They had great slides.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.640
It's just the machine gun
might get you on the way down.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:30.820
[Laughter]

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.360
This is turning dark! So let's move on.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.860
North Butlins and South Butlins!

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:38.480
'Get on the slide!'
[Machine gun noises]

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.180
No, no, you've got the breakaway
Republic of Pontins, above.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:42.360
[Laughter]

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.580
Blue coats — red coats. They don't talk. Don't talk.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:48.960
Your last question then.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:49.620
Aww.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.700
Baseball and bridge.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.560
We are looking for something they have in common.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.060
CHRIS: An extra —
GARY: Bats!

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:58.020
An extra card in the box.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.140
GARY: The way I play it.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.900
So first of all, that's quite clever, but no.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.680
And secondly, what bat do you use in bridge?!

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.600
Cricket, and it speeds it up.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.560
[Laughter]

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:14.800
TOM: That's fair.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.400
Was there ever an iron baseball?

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.640
Because you could say both have
been designed by Brunel.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:23.780
[Laughter]

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:27.220
GARY: 'This ball is superior to all others!' Thonk!

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.480
It's also slightly too big for everything else as well.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.360
TOM: Yeah. No, we are looking for terms...

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:34.140
I'll give you a point for one of the terms that is found —

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.300
MATT: Terms of endearment?

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:36.040
...in both.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:36.740
GARY: Can I have a go?

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:37.560
Yeah, go for it.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.260
Rubber?

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:39.460
[DING]
GARY: Thank you...

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.540
Absolutely spot on. It's 'Which two games
have both a rubber and a grand slam?'

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.920
And that is such a good bit of obscure knowledge that...

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:49.920
[Applause]

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.780
Because I like, despite all the gags,

00:09:55.780 --> 00:10:01.100
that we've ended on Gary coming out with a really
obscure bit of knowledge and absolutely nailing it.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:02.780
MATT: Shall I save this?

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.300
[Blows raspberry]

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:05.700
[Laughter]

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.680
Nailed it. Nailed it.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.480
But at the end of that, it is clearly
Chris that wins, so congratulations.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:12.800
[Applause]

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:32.640
Congratulations! You win an enclosure in which
underwear is forbidden, run by the star of Face/Off.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:33.980
Really!

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:35.560
GARY: No! Don't do this!

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:37.140
I've worked it out. Don't do this, Tom!

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:37.940
Gary, take it.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.340
Knickerless Cage?

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.260
TOM: It's Nicolas Cage's Knickerless Cage.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.900
[Laughter and applause]

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.200
With that, for the final time tonight...

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.080
Please give it up for Chris Joel!

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:52.800
Gary Brannan!

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:55.460
Matt Gray!

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.020
TOM: I've been Tom Scott —
MATT: And Tom Scott!

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:00.400
[Cheering]

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.680
Thank you! Goodnight!

